# setup.sh copies this file to deploy/.env and fills in HONCHO_ROOT; or copy it by hand. The defaults work as
# they are; set HONCHO_WORKSPACE, and change the rest only to move something. Relative paths are read from this
# deploy/ folder: the defaults are the folders shipped beside src/, whose contents git ignores.
# Commented lines are optional; the value shown is what the code uses when the line is absent.

# Which of this host's addresses the dashboard (port 8010) listens on. Empty = all of them. Set one (e.g. the LAN
# address) to keep the page off the others, such as a VPN or a second network port. You open the page at
# http://<host address>:8010 either way.
BIND_IP=

# The Honcho project folder (Honcho's own compose project). Only two of its files are used:
# - .env, Honcho's secrets and the provider keys, loaded by every service through env_file (read on the
#   host, so no key ever becomes a file inside a container);
# - config.toml, bound as one file into the dashboard at /honcho/config.toml (read-write: the dashboard
#   edits it in place; Honcho keeps reading it from its own folder).
# No container mounts the whole folder.
HONCHO_ROOT=/path/to/honcho

# The config folder: settings.json and models.json, and backups/ (a timestamped copy of settings.json,
# models.json, config.toml, the instruction files and the workspace configuration before every save).
# Mounted at /config: read-write for the dashboard and the scheduler (peer discovery appends new peers
# to settings.json), read-only for the bridge and Honcho's api and deriver. The folder is mounted, never
# settings.json alone: a bind of the file keeps the old inode after a temp-file rename, and hot reload
# never sees the new file.
CONFIG_DIR=../config

# The data folder: instructions/ (the dream and peer-card instruction files), nothing else. Mounted at
# /data in the dashboard; the deriver mounts its instructions/ folder read-only (see honcho-mounts.yaml).
DATA_DIR=../data

# The logs folder. Every component writes its logs here and nowhere else: policy-used.log, runs.log
# and hook-status.json (the deriver hook, through its own mount; see honcho-mounts.yaml),
# bridge-calls.jsonl (bridge), restart.log (dashboard), dream-nas.log (scheduler), maintenance.log
# (telemetry), backup.log (backup). Mounted at /logs.
LOGS_DIR=../logs

# Telemetry event files and Postgres dump paths. Where this stack keeps Honcho’s telemetry events and the nightly database dumps.
TELEMETRY_DIR=../telemetry
BACKUP_DIR=../backups

# The stack's private folder: tokens.env (BRIDGE_TOKEN, TELEMETRY_TOKEN, TELEMETRY_HEADERS, written once by
# setup.sh, loaded by every service and by Honcho's api and deriver) and the bridge's CLI logins: codex/ (Codex
# auth.json, copied from a machine where `codex login` ran) and claude/
# (written by `claude login` run once inside the bridge container). The default cli-secrets/ beside src/ is
# ignored by git; it must be writable by the container's user (uid 1000). Keep it private: these are
# subscription logins. Mounted at /home/node/.codex and /home/node/.claude.
SECRETS_DIR=../cli-secrets

# Supplementary group for the bridge (uid 1000), only for a LOGS_DIR or SECRETS_DIR that one group alone may write
# (a NAS share, say): that group's numeric id (`stat -c %g <folder>` on the host). Not needed when setup.sh gave
# both folders to uid 1000, the default.
BRIDGE_LOG_GID=1000

# CONTROL_FILE is not set here. Compose sets it to /config/settings.json inside each container
# (the file inside CONFIG_DIR). Do not point a volume at a host path named CONTROL_FILE.
# DOCKER_PROXY_URL is not set here either. Compose sets it to http://docker-proxy:2375.

# Honcho API as seen from the compose network. The token (HONCHO_API_TOKEN) stays in Honcho's .env.
HONCHO_API_URL=http://api:8000
# The Honcho workspace the scheduler and the dashboard's peer pages work on. One workspace per stack: in
# scheduler mode the hook holds every dream the scheduler did not start, so other workspaces do not dream.
# Planned support for multiple workspaces not implemented yet.
HONCHO_WORKSPACE=

# Docker network of the Honcho stack (compose project name + _default).
HONCHO_NETWORK=honcho_default

# Dashboard login (HTTP basic). Set CONTROL_PASSWORD in Honcho's .env to require it; blank or unset leaves the
# page open with no login. CONTROL_USER defaults to admin.
CONTROL_USER=admin

# Telemetry receiver. Names only; the process has defaults for the empty ones.
ALLOWED_SOURCES=
MIN_FREE_GB=2
GZIP_AFTER_DAYS=1
RETENTION_DAYS=90
# Largest accepted post in MiB (default 64). Keep it above Honcho's largest telemetry batch: a refused batch
# is sent again at every flush and blocks the ones behind it. See src/telemetry/README.md.
MAX_BODY_MB=
VIEWER_URL=
VIEWER_KEY=

# Backups. BACKUP_AT is read in the TZ zone. Set your local timezone for backups.
# List timezones with `timedatectl list-timezones`
TZ=UTC
BACKUP_AT=03:30
KEEP_DAILY=7
KEEP_WEEKLY=4
# Attempts per nightly run (default 8) and the first wait between them in seconds (default 30, doubling up
# to 15 minutes). A wrong password or database name is not retried.
BACKUP_ATTEMPTS=
BACKUP_RETRY_SECONDS=

# The Honcho database, for the scheduler and the backup. POSTGRES_PASSWORD stays in the Honcho .env,
# which compose loads first. The defaults are the service name database, user honcho and database
# honcho; Honcho's own compose example uses user and database postgres, so set PGUSER and PGDATABASE
# to what your database service uses.
# PGHOST=database
# PGPORT=5432
# PGUSER=honcho
# PGDATABASE=honcho

# Optional full API prefix, including /v3. When unset, the scheduler uses HONCHO_API_URL + /v3.
# HONCHO_API=

# Bridge image: 1 installs that CLI at the latest version at build, 0 leaves it out.
INSTALL_CODEX=0
INSTALL_CLAUDE=0

# Bridge process. The bridge is not published on the host; Honcho reaches it as
# http://bridge:8793/v1 on the Honcho network.
# BRIDGE_PORT=8793
# BRIDGE_EXE=codex
# BRIDGE_CLAUDE=claude
# BRIDGE_EXE may be a JSON list (an interpreter plus a script) when the executable is a wrapper.
# BRIDGE_CONCURRENCY and BRIDGE_TIMEOUT are fallbacks. providers.concurrency and providers.timeout_seconds win.


# No secret lives in this file. Every service also loads two secrets files. ${HONCHO_ROOT}/.env, the Honcho
# project's .env: POSTGRES_PASSWORD, AUTH_JWT_SECRET, HONCHO_API_TOKEN, CONTROL_PASSWORD, VIEWER_KEY if the viewer
# uses one, RERANK_API_KEY (read by Honcho's api and deriver for the rerank component), and the provider keys named
# by settings.json key_env (OPENROUTER_API_KEY, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, ...). ${SECRETS_DIR}/tokens.env, written once by
# setup.sh: BRIDGE_TOKEN (the bridge requires it; Honcho's bridge model blocks name it in api_key_env),
# TELEMETRY_TOKEN and TELEMETRY_HEADERS.
